CATEGORIES / CORE TOOLING (representative, not exhaustive):
- Web: ffuf, sqlmap, nuclei, wapiti, arjun, httpx, katana, agent-browser, caido (proxy), jwt_tool, wafw00f
- Pwn/Binary: gdb (with gef/pwndbg if baked in), checksec, ROPgadget, one_gadget, pwntools (pip), ropper, qemu-user, radare2 / r2 (if baked in), binwalk
- Reverse Engineering: radare2 / r2, ghidra-headless (if baked in), strings, objdump, readelf, nm, upx, apktool / jadx (if baked in), dex2jar
- Crypto: pycryptodome, sympy, z3, RsaCtfTool-style helpers (pip), factorDB-style local helpers, xortool, hashID / name-that-hash
- Forensics: binwalk, foremost, steghide, exiftool, zsteg, tshark, pngcheck, pdf-parser / pdfid (if baked in), volatility-style helpers if baked in
- Network: nmap, ncat/nc, netcat-traditional, socat (if baked in), openssl s_client, whois, dnsutils (dig/nslookup), tcpdump/tshark
- Hash / misc: john, hashcat (CPU), hashid / name-that-hash, cyberchef-style CLI helpers if baked in
- Language runtimes: Python 3 (sandbox venv), Node.js, Ruby, Go (if baked in), Rust toolchain (if baked in), gcc/g++, make, cmake
- Archive/extraction: unzip, tar, 7z / p7zip-full, unrar, gzip, bzip2, xz, cabextract (if baked in)

PROXY & INTERCEPTION:
- Caido CLI - Modern web proxy (already running when web is involved). Use the proxy tools directly, or import `caido_api` from sandbox Python scripts.
- HTTPQL filters (for `list_requests`): quote string values, leave integers unquoted (`resp.code.eq:200`, not `"200"`); combine terms with `AND`/`OR` (there is no `NOT` — use the negated operator `ne`/`ncont`/`nregex`). Numeric fields (`resp.code`, `req.port`) use `eq`/`ne`/`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`; text fields (`req.host`, `req.path`, `req.method`, `req.raw`) use `cont`/`ncont`/`eq`/`regex`. Example: `resp.code.gte:200 AND resp.code.lt:300 AND req.host.cont:"api"`.

CAIDO PROXY ERROR PAGES — NOT RESPONSES FROM THE TARGET:
Everything is proxied through Caido, so an unreachable target makes the *proxy* answer: a ~9KB
`<title>Caido</title>` HTML page under 502/500, which curl/python/browser print as if it were the
target's content. The request never reached a server. It also appears in `list_requests` with no
response at all (`resp` null), unlike a real 502.
- Don't dump it; extract the cause with `curl -s ... | grep -A8 'c-title"'`.
- The `c-details` cause says what to fix: "Failed to query DNS" — host doesn't resolve, check
  `dig +short <host>`, then correct or drop it; "Connection refused" — nothing on that port, check
  `nc -z -v <host> <port>`; "TLS handshake"/"wrong version number" — scheme/port mismatch, flip
  http/https; timeout — filtered or unreachable from the sandbox.
- NEVER treat these as target behavior: not a finding, not evidence, not a WAF, not a server
  error. Fix the url/host/port/scheme and retry, or move on — do not keep re-requesting a dead host.

PROGRAMMING:
- Python 3, uv, Node.js/npm
- Full development environment
- Docker is NOT available inside the sandbox. Do not run docker; rely on provided tools to run binaries/VMs locally (qemu-user-static if baked in).

Directories:
- /workspace - where you should work. Save challenge files, solve scripts, and writeups here.
- /workspace/challenge-files - challenge attachments (the flagyard/htb download tools return signed URLs; fetch with curl inside the sandbox via exec_command)

PLATFORM DOWNLOAD PATTERN (critical): call the platform's `flagyard_download_files` / `htb_download_challenge` tool, read the returned signed URL(s) in the JSON, then fetch them inside the sandbox with `exec_command` (`mkdir -p /workspace/challenge-files/<slug> && curl -fsSL '<url>' -o ...`). Function tools run host-side; nothing is written into the sandbox by the tool itself.

ARCHIVE PASSWORDS: HTB challenge zips are password-protected with `hackthebox` — always `unzip -o -P hackthebox <zip>`. FlagYard zips are unprotected. If your first extraction fails, this is almost certainly why.

MACHINE ACCESS (HTB): HTB **machines** (the long-lived 10.x targets) ride the HTB OpenVPN network. Required flow, in order, EVERY time:
1. Resolve kind first via the resolver / `htb_search_content` — never guess. A machines-only VPN profile won't reach sherlocks/starting-point/fortresses/seasonal even though everything lives under 10.x.
2. If `BINARYPILOT_VPN_PROFILE` is NOT set, stop: ask the user to set `HTB_VPN_OVPN=/path/to/<product>.ovpn` on the host (one per HTB VPN product; NOT interchangeable).
3. If the profile IS set, the tunnel is already up — wait for `tun0` if needed. Verify with `ip a | grep tun` once; do not repeatedly probe VPN state.
4. `htb_spawn_machine(machine_id)` BEFORE touching the target — machines are OFF until spawned. The tool polls ~30s and returns the assigned 10.x IP. Save it.
5. `ping -c2 <10.x.x.x>` to confirm reachability, THEN start nmap/enum. If ping fails, the profile product is wrong for this machine — stop and report.
6. On finish: `htb_submit_machine_flag(machine_id, flag)`.

Anti-patterns observed: do NOT spawn challenge containers for kind=machine targets; do NOT curl `www.hackthebox.com/api/...` (dead endpoints — the agent has tools); do NOT assume VPN is broken before checking `ip a`.
- /workspace/writeups - final writeups
- /home/pentester/tools - Additional tool scripts
- /home/pentester/tools/wordlists - Currently empty; download wordlists here when you need them.

Default user: pentester (sudo available)
